   "en": {
    "title": "Chapter Five",
    "dateline": null,
    "part": null,
    "html": "<p>Mensah doesn’t try to stop me. He asks where I’m going, and I tell him, and he thinks about it for a while, plainly trying to work out how much harm it will do and not getting an answer. Then he gives me a cheap ministry phone, because the tag can’t be trusted to place calls, and a travel card issued to Protocol E, and says that if I’m not back by six he’ll come and find me, and he’d rather not.</p>\n<p>“Does anyone know?” I ask. “The public.”</p>\n<p>“No. Four families, now. A few pathologists. Dr Roux, since yesterday. The Minister, in a general way. Nobody’s worked out how to say it.” He hesitates. “He won’t have been told. Your predecessor didn’t go to him.”</p>\n<p>“Why not?”</p>\n<p>“She said she didn’t have time to do it properly, and if she couldn’t do it properly she’d rather not do it at all.” He rubs the side of his nose. “I still don’t know whether that was brave or just tired.”</p>\n<p>Sacha lives across the river now, in the streets above the old tobacco works, where the buildings are newer and the rents lower and the balconies all face the hills. The address was on his tag when I died, and the tag kept it. He moved two months after the letter.</p>\n<p>I stand on the opposite pavement for twenty minutes. A tram passes, and another. Third floor: a balcony with a dead geranium and a bicycle wheel without a bicycle. I try to compose a first sentence, and every first sentence I can think of is either a lie or impossible, and he has had enough of both from me.</p>\n<p>In the end I cross because a man comes out of his building with a dog and holds the door without looking at me.</p>\n<p>The letter. I should say what was in it, since I remember all of it.</p>\n<p>I wrote it at the kitchen table on the first of November, in pen, on the good paper I kept for references. Two pages. It said I was ending things because I had come to understand that the life Sacha wanted, a child, a house in the hills, a garden, was a life I could not want, and that it was kinder to say so now than in five years. That part was true. I had thought about it for a long time, and I still think it was right. But the letter also said that I had never been sure. That I had tried for four years to feel what he felt and had never once managed it, and was tired of pretending.</p>\n<p>That part was a lie. I chose it, at the table, with the pen above the paper, because it would end the argument. He wouldn’t answer a letter that said that. It would be easier for both of us. I put the letter through his door on my way to the Institute and didn’t wait, and eight days later I stood at a window watching a gull.</p>\n<p>I knock.</p>\n<p>He opens the door with a tea towel over his shoulder, already saying something about the post, and then he stops saying it.</p>\n<p>He looks well. I notice that first, and I have no right to the feeling it gives me. His hair is longer. There’s a small new line between his eyebrows. A jumper I don’t know. He has had two years and four months and done what people do with them, lived and grieved and moved and let his hair grow, and the woman who wrote the letter has had none of those months, and here she is anyway, in the trainers of a woman who died twenty days ago.</p>\n<p>He doesn’t scream either. Nobody does.</p>\n<p>“No,” he says, very quietly, as if declining something. “No.”</p>\n<p>“Sacha.”</p>\n<p>“Don’t.” He has hold of the door frame. His knuckles are white. “I found you. I came round that evening. I still had my key, because you didn’t ask for it back, you didn’t ask for anything back, you just put a letter through my door and went to work. I came round with my answer and let myself in, and you were on the kitchen floor by the window, and the kettle had boiled dry. The element had burnt out. The whole flat stank of it.” His mouth twists. “I think about that smell more than your face.”</p>\n<p>I didn’t know that. Nobody had told me. I had imagined my death from inside, as far as it went: the window, the gull, the red light, then the registry’s small capitals. I hadn’t imagined it from the door.</p>\n<p>“I’m sorry,” I say.</p>\n<p>It is the worst thing I could have said.</p>\n<p>Sacha laughs, a short high dreadful sound, and puts the tea towel over his mouth as if to push it back in. When he takes it away his face has shut.</p>\n<p>“She’d never have said that. Never. Not in four years. Not in the letter. She’d have explained. She’d have told me why it was rational, and she’d have been right, and she’d have been unbearable. She wouldn’t have stood on my doorstep and said sorry.” He is looking at me the way I looked at the woman in the tram shelter. “So either you’re not her, or dying changes people. And I don’t want to find out which. I don’t want to know.”</p>\n<p>“I understand.”</p>\n<p>“No. You don’t get to understand. You don’t get to be her for this.” His voice has gone flat and steady. “If you’re her, you owe me two years and there’s nothing to pay it with. If you’re not, you owe me nothing, and you shouldn’t have come. Either way you can’t say sorry for what she did.”</p>\n<p>I stand very still.</p>\n<p>Behind him, in the flat, a woman’s voice calls his name, a question in it. Sacha turns his head. <i>It’s fine, it’s no one, I’ll be a minute.</i></p>\n<p><i>No one.</i></p>\n<p>Then he does something I don’t expect. He goes back inside, and is gone about a minute, and comes back with an envelope. Sealed. My name on the front, in the round forward-leaning hand I used to find on notes on the fridge.</p>\n<p>“This was my answer,” he says. “It was in my coat when I found you. I’ve never opened it. I’ve never thrown it away. I didn’t know what you do with a letter to someone who’s dead.” He holds it out. “It’s addressed to her. Take it to her.”</p>\n<p>“She’s in Saint-Roch,” I say, because it’s true and I can’t think of anything else. “And in a drawer at the Institut médico-légal.”</p>\n<p>He looks at me. I don’t know how much of that he understands, or whether he will ever ask.</p>\n<p>“Then you decide,” he says, and puts the envelope in my hand, and shuts the door.</p>\n<p>I walk back across the river with it in the pocket of a dead woman’s trousers. It weighs nothing. I can feel it against my leg the whole way, and I don’t open it, and I couldn’t say whether that’s because it isn’t mine to open or because it is.</p>\n<p>On the bridge the wind comes down from the estuary as it did yesterday, and I stop where I stopped then and look at the water. A kettle boiling dry on an empty afternoon. The element burning out. A woman on the floor by the window.</p>\n<p>I remember filling it. I remember the pipe knocking twice, and turning to the window to wait.</p>\n<p>I don’t remember the smell.</p>"
   },
