   "en": {
    "title": "Chapter Twenty-Six",
    "dateline": null,
    "part": {
     "num": "Three",
     "title": "Editions",
     "dateline": "Ceto, the two hundred and eleventh year of the Reception",
     "epi": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p class=\"fr\"><i>Ainsi chacun de nous a vécu, vit et vivra sans fin, sous forme de milliards d’alter ego.</i></p><p>So each of us has lived, lives and will live without end, in the form of billions of other selves.</p></blockquote>"
    },
    "html": "<p>The last thing I remember is Sacha’s thumb stopping.</p>\n<p>On <i>two</i>. I remember thinking he was afraid it would hurt. Somewhere, a man of seventy with a bad knee is getting up from a folding chair in a stone room at the end of a naval quay and going home on the tram to a flat where there’s nobody. I won’t ever know whether he cried in the car.</p>\n<p>It doesn’t hurt. Nothing hurts. I’m sitting in a chair.</p>\n<p>I open my eyes.</p>\n<p>Four metres by five by three. Grey stone walls. An oak floor. A window of a particular size in a particular wall, a chair in the middle of the room with me in it, my right hand on the plate on the chair arm, and under my palm a sheet of paper folded twice.</p>\n<p>The same room. Elias drew it to the millimetre so it would be.</p>\n<p>I do the drill anyway. <i>First, do not move. Second, check that you can breathe.</i> The air is cold and thin and tastes faintly of metal, and when I breathe deeply something in my chest objects and then settles. <i>Third, check the time.</i> There’s no tag in my arm. I press the place and there’s nothing under the skin but forearm. <i>Fourth, do not assume anything about where you are.</i></p>\n<p>I look at the window.</p>\n<p>It faces south, as the specification required, the right size, at the right height, and the light that comes through it isn’t the long gold light of an evening over the estuary. It’s violet. It comes from a small white sun very low in a sky the colour of an old bruise, and it lies across the oak in a thin cold bar, and beyond the window, under that sky, there is a sea.</p>\n<p>A dark sea, nearly black, very flat, running out to a horizon that’s closer than it should be. And above the horizon, filling a quarter of the sky, a planet. Banded cream and rust and pale blue, with rings seen almost edge-on, one thin bright line ruled across the violet. It is so large and so close and so silent that I sit with my hand on the plate and forget to breathe.</p>\n<p class=\"doc\"><i>You will think you know where you are. You will be wrong in ways you can’t see.</i></p>\n<p>I can see this one perfectly well.</p>\n<p>Someone built this room exactly. Someone under a ringed planet, on the shore of a black sea, cut grey stone and oak to the right dimensions and set the window to the south because a description told them to, and the description was true here, so here I am. As well as everywhere else. Arun showed me the number the evening before Sacha sat down beside me. I’m the one who woke in this room.</p>\n<p>I unfold the paper. It’s come through coarse, as they said, but it’s come through. The blue door. The lime tree. Three people and a dog we don’t have. The tiny figure a long way off, waving. <i>POUR MAMAN. POUR LE VOYAGE.</i></p>\n<p>I fold it and button it into my shirt pocket and put my hand over it. Then I stand, slowly, because the gravity is wrong, lighter, so that standing feels like the top of a stair when you expect one more step, and I go to the window and put my free hand on the cold glass.</p>\n<p>Behind me a door opens.</p>\n<p>I didn’t know the room had a door. Elias’s specification didn’t include one; he said the receiving world would supply its own. This one has supplied it in the wall behind the chair, a plain panel of the same stone, and it has slid aside without a sound, and there’s a person in the doorway.</p>\n<p>Tall, taller than anyone I know, with the long narrow bones of someone grown up in light gravity, in a loose garment of undyed grey cloth. Hair cropped close and very white, though the face is young. A cup in each hand, both steaming.</p>\n<p>They look at me for a while, quite calmly. They’ve done this before.</p>\n<p>“Good evening,” they say. The language is French, nearly. The vowels are longer and flatter and some of the consonants have gone soft in a way I half know from the hills behind my grandmother’s village; I understand it by ear, a beat behind. “Welcome to Ceto. My name is Tavi. I’m the receiver on duty.” They hold out one of the cups. “It’s tea. It’ll taste wrong. Everything does for the first few days.”</p>\n<p>It tastes wrong. I drink it anyway.</p>\n<p>The cup is warm all the way round, not just where the tea touches, and it’s made of something grey and faintly porous, like pumice that has learned manners. Through the door behind Tavi I can hear the sea, and the sea is wrong too: the waves come in too slowly, with too long a gap between them, as if the water here were heavier or the world lighter. Tavi says something I don’t catch, a phrase like <i>you are welcome to the floor</i>. I nod as if I’d understood. I find out later it means: sit down before you fall.</p>\n<p>“Thank you,” I say.</p>\n<p>Tavi smiles, a small exact smile. “You said thank you. Most editions don’t, for the first hour.”</p>\n<p>“Editions.”</p>\n<p>“That’s what we call you. The ones who arrive.” They gesture with their cup at the chair, the plate, the window. “This is Reception Nine, on the south shore. There are forty-one on Ceto and four hundred and six in the system. They’ve been receiving for two hundred and eleven years.” A pause. “May I ask the name of the one who was recorded?”</p>\n<p>“Who chose the word? Editions.”</p>\n<p>“The first ones. As a joke, I think. Printers’ language.” Tavi hesitates. “Not everyone likes it. An edition can be corrected. Revised. Superseded by a better one. Pulped. There are people in Port-Taureau who won’t let you call them that to their face.”</p>\n<p>Elias and his drill. The woman on the clinic steps. A man in a mended jacket watching me instead of the screen.</p>\n<p>“Inès Arrighi,” I say.</p>\n<p>Tavi takes a small flat object out of the folds of the grey cloth and looks at it, and something in their face shifts, very slightly, like a reader’s when a book does something it has done before.</p>\n<p>“Arrighi,” they say. “Yes. You’ll want to sit down again, I think.”</p>\n<p>“Why?”</p>\n<p>“Because you’re not the first,” Tavi says. “Ceto has received Inès Arrighi four thousand two hundred and eleven times. You’re the four thousand two hundred and twelfth.”</p>"
   },
