    "part": null,
    "html": "<p>Nès is right. It’s the second day.</p>\n<p>The registry hall in Port-Taureau is a long cool building with a ceiling of pale timber and a floor worn smooth by two hundred years of people who weren’t supposed to exist. I’m at one of the reading desks with Tavi beside me. I’ve typed a name into the search, and the registry has thought for a moment and given me an answer, and I’m holding the edge of the desk.</p>\n<p>I hadn’t meant to look her up. I’d told myself, in the narrow room above Reception Nine with Thalassa filling the window, that I wouldn’t. Mira is nine. She’s in a world I’ll never see again, eating breakfast, complaining about the tablet her teacher makes them use, with a mother who went home from the white room. She doesn’t need me. That was the whole argument.</p>\n<p>But there’s a reading desk every few metres, and people stand at them quietly, editions and the children of editions, typing names, and on the second morning I stood at one and typed hers before I’d decided anything.</p>\n<p>MIRA LIND-ARRIGHI.</p>\n<p>EDITION. RECEIVED RECEPTION THIRTY-ONE, NORTH SHORE, YEAR 173.</p>\n<p>AGE AT RECEPTION: 31.</p>\n<p>STATUS: LIVING.</p>\n<p>“Year one seventy-three,” Tavi says, reading over my shoulder. “Thirty-eight years ago.”</p>\n<p>Thirty-one when she arrived. Thirty-eight years.</p>\n<p>She’s sixty-nine.</p>\n<p>My daughter is sixty-nine and lives on a moon, and I’m fifty-one, and I got here yesterday.</p>\n<p>She lives on the north shore, in a house at the end of a long spit of black shingle with the sea on both sides. Tavi drives me in a small open vehicle that hums, along a coast road with the planet hanging over the water on our left. I don’t say anything the whole way. Tavi doesn’t ask me to.</p>\n<p>The house is low and grey, built of the same stone as the Receptions, with a garden of some tough blue-green plant that grows in mounds between the rocks. A woman is kneeling in the garden, jabbing at something with a short-handled fork and swearing at it. She sits back on her heels when she hears the vehicle and pushes her hair off her face with the back of her wrist.</p>\n<p>Grey hair, short. Sacha’s face, the long upper lip and the straight dark brows, aged. My eyes. And a stubborn set of the jaw that is entirely her own and that I last saw on a nine-year-old at eleven at night in pyjamas, saying <i>then take something</i>. She’s wearing a man’s cardigan too big for her, with the elbows gone.</p>\n<p>She gets up, slowly, a hand on her knee, and looks at me across the blue-green mounds for a while.</p>\n<p>“Hello, Maman,” she says.</p>\n<p>I can’t speak.</p>\n<p>“You’re the twenty-third,” Mira says. “Of you. That I’ve met. I check the registry every Thursday. I go to them or they come here. Most of them don’t last.” She pulls off her gardening gloves a finger at a time. “You look like the one from Reception Nine. They usually do. Wipe your feet. The sea gets into everything.”</p>\n<p>Inside it’s warm and smells of earth and something baking, and there are books everywhere, stacked on chairs, and on the kitchen wall, framed, there’s a drawing.</p>\n<p>A house on a hill with a blue door and a lime tree. Three people and a dog. Across the top in felt-tip, in a round forward-leaning hand: <i>POUR MAMAN. POUR LE VOYAGE.</i></p>\n<p>I stand in front of it.</p>\n<p>“One of you brought it,” Mira says behind me. “The fourth, I think. Or the fifth. She came through holding it. Coarse, they always are, but you could still read it.” She’s putting a kettle on an iron stove. “She gave it to me before she stopped. She said it was mine. I said it was hers. She said that was the point. I thought that was a bit much, frankly. I framed it anyway.”</p>\n<p>I take the drawing out of my shirt pocket and unfold it.</p>\n<p>Mira turns from the stove and sees it, and stops.</p>\n<p>The same drawing. Mine’s coarser; it’s a copy of a copy. The lines are a little thicker and the blue of the door has bled very slightly into the paper. But the tiny figure, a long way from the house, waving, is in the same place on both.</p>\n<p>“I drew that,” Mira says. Quite steadily. “I was nine. I took the paper out of Papa’s drawer, the good paper he kept for letters. He was furious. And then I gave it to you in the car on the way to the white room, and you held it under your hand.” She comes and stands beside me, looking at the two of them. “I watched you go in. I wasn’t supposed to; they said children shouldn’t. I watched through the glass. Then you came out and we went home and you made pancakes, and you were exactly the same. You were my mother for twenty-one more years.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“You don’t know.” No heat in it. “How could you? You left in the white room. She’s the one who stayed.” She takes the drawing out of my hands, looks at it, gives it back. “She died when I was thirty. Cancer. Quick. And afterwards I couldn’t stop thinking about the other one. The one who went. The little figure a long way off, waving. I kept thinking, she’s somewhere and she’s on her own and nobody’s come.” She folds her arms. “So I volunteered. The programme was still going. They wanted people who’d lost someone to the sends; they said we’d make good receivers. I was recorded with the Reception’s description, and I came through here, and I’ve been here thirty-eight years, and every Thursday I check the registry for you.”</p>\n<p>“Twenty-three times.”</p>\n<p>“Twenty-three.” Nearly a smile. “None of you are her. I’m not a fool. I helped redraft the Concord in one-ninety. You’re not my mother. You’re a woman with her past who came through a room yesterday holding a drawing I did when I was nine.” The kettle starts to sing. She leaves it. “And I sit with every one of you, for as long as you last. I make tea. I complain about the garden. Some of you lasted an hour. One lasted four years; she’s buried on the spit, under the cairn.”</p>\n<p>She looks at the kettle, and decides something.</p>\n<p>“I’ll tell you something I haven’t told the others. I’d decided you were the last. Before I knew you’d come. When this one goes, I said, I stop checking.” She doesn’t look at me. “And I liked her better. The four-year one. She was rude, and she was kind, and she never once asked me to be the girl in the drawing. I still go and talk to her cairn, which she’d have thought was ridiculous.” More quietly: “Sorry. That’s not fair. You’ve been here a day.”</p>\n<p>“It’s fair,” I say. It is. It still hurts.</p>\n<p>“Tavi will give you the handbook,” she says, taking the kettle off at last. “The receivers’ handbook. Two hundred years of things editions brought through and said. There’s a page about a widow on a slipway who made coffee for her returned husband for thirty-one hours. People love that page. Somebody’s written in the margin that it’s the only answer anyone’s ever found.” She snorts. “It isn’t an answer. It’s a recipe for coffee. I use it every time.”</p>\n<p>She makes tea, two cups, too much milk, without asking how I take it.</p>\n<p>“Sit down, Maman. And don’t tell me about the garden, I know about the garden. Tell me about the other world. Tell me what she was like, the one who sent you, the dying one. I’ve never met one of you who came through somebody else.”</p>\n<p>I sit. The tea tastes wrong; everything does, for the first few days.</p>\n<p>I drink it anyway, and I tell her.</p>"
